Model-agnostic ``plug-and-play'' modules for time series forecasting---instance
normalization, non-stationarity correction, frequency-domain losses---are reported
as helpful, yet their gain flips sign across backbones. The natural response is to
learn \emph{when} to attach \emph{which} module. In $2{,}035$ single-GPU training
runs across four backbones, eight datasets and four horizons, three selector
families did not convert that promise into a deployable gain. First, the effect is
smaller than three routine reporting choices---complete-block filtering, the error
metric, and mean versus median---each of which flips a verdict. Second, the
per-window oracle motivating gating is provably invariant to re-labeling which arm
produced which error, so its $+10.15\%$ headroom is no evidence of learnability,
although $79\%$ of it survives a seed change. Third, best-arm identity has a median
correlation half-life of $5.1\%$ of the horizon, whereas deployment forces a
decision lag of at least one horizon. The selectors lose to the arm frozen on
validation, by $0.87$ and $0.67$ points, and are absolutely worse in most groups.
With the eight datasets as the unit of generalization, the gating loss survives
clustering while the online loss no longer excludes zero. Only a constant
mis-selection was recoverable; we distill the audit into a checklist.
